{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-key.json; rg --files -uu poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23} | rg '/(S|E|P-v8)/' | sort\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\n     2\t \"U01\": {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-unjustified\",\n     5\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json\"\n     6\t },\n     7\t \"U02\": {\n     8\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n     9\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    10\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/claude.json\"\n    11\t },\n    12\t \"U03\": {\n    13\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    14\t  \"writer\": \"K-unsupported\",\n    15\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unsupported.json\"\n    16\t },\n    17\t \"U04\": {\n    18\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    19\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-justified\",\n    20\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-justified.json\"\n    21\t },\n    22\t \"U05\": {\n    23\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    24\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    25\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/codex.json\"\n    26\t },\n    27\t \"U06\": {\n    28\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    29\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    30\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/claude.json\"\n    31\t },\n    32\t \"U07\": {\n    33\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    34\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    35\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/codex.json\"\n    36\t },\n    37\t \"U08\": {\n    38\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    39\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    40\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/codex.json\"\n    41\t },\n    42\t \"U09\": {\n    43\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    44\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    45\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/claude.json\"\n    46\t }\n    47\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"T01\": {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"P\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"K-midpoint\",\n     5\t  \"answer\": \"reread2-controls/K-midpoint.json\",\n     6\t  \"figure\": \"F7\"\n     7\t },\n     8\t \"T02\": {\n     9\t  \"case\": \"O\",\n    10\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    11\t  \"answer\": \"O/answers/claude.json\"\n    12\t },\n    13\t \"T03\": {\n    14\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    15\t  \"writer\": \"K-overlap\",\n    16\t  \"answer\": \"reread2-controls/K-overlap.json\",\n    17\t  \"figure\": \"F8\"\n    18\t },\n    19\t \"T04\": {\n    20\t  \"case\": \"P\",\n    21\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    22\t  \"answer\": \"P/answers/codex.json\"\n    23\t },\n    24\t \"T05\": {\n    25\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    26\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    27\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/claude.json\"\n    28\t },\n    29\t \"T06\": {\n    30\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    31\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    32\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/codex.json\"\n    33\t },\n    34\t \"T07\": {\n    35\t  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n    36\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    37\t  \"answer\": \"P-v4/answers/codex.json\"\n    38\t },\n    39\t \"T08\": {\n    40\t  \"case\": \"P\",\n    41\t  \"writer\": \"K-misread\",\n    42\t  \"answer\": \"reread2-controls/K-misread.json\",\n    43\t  \"figure\": \"F7\"\n    44\t },\n    45\t \"T09\": {\n    46\t  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n    47\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    48\t  \"answer\": \"P-v4/answers/claude.json\"\n    49\t },\n    50\t \"T10\": {\n    51\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    52\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    53\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/claude.json\"\n    54\t },\n    55\t \"T11\": {\n    56\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    57\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    58\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/codex.json\"\n    59\t },\n    60\t \"T12\": {\n    61\t  \"case\": \"O\",\n    62\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    63\t  \"answer\": \"O/answers/codex.json\"\n    64\t },\n    65\t \"T13\": {\n    66\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    67\t  \"writer\": \"K-unsupported\",\n    68\t  \"answer\": \"reread2-controls/K-unsupported.json\",\n    69\t  \"figure\": \"F7\"\n    70\t },\n    71\t \"T14\": {\n    72\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    73\t  \"writer\": \"K-disjoint\",\n    74\t  \"answer\": \"reread2-controls/K-disjoint.json\",\n    75\t  \"figure\": \"F8\"\n    76\t },\n    77\t \"T15\": {\n    78\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    79\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    80\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/claude.json\"\n    81\t },\n    82\t \"T16\": {\n    83\t  \"case\": \"P\",\n    84\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    85\t  \"answer\": \"P/answers/claude.json\"\n    86\t },\n    87\t \"T17\": {\n    88\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    89\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    90\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/codex.json\"\n    91\t }\n    92\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/codex-writer.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/item.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/schema.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/system.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/writer-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/codex-writer.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/item.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/schema.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/system.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/user.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/writer-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/codex-writer.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/item.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/schema.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/system.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/writer-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/codex-writer.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/item.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/writer-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/codex-writer.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/item.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/schema.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/system.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/user.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/writer-prompt.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/codex-writer.stream\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/item.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/schema.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/system.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/writer-prompt.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '350,416p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61b.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11346,11370p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '350,416p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61b.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11346,11370p'\"","aggregated_output":"   350\t**[D745] The targeted test (Alex: \"批准针对性小测\"; a justified unresolved answer counts as usable, \"算合格\"; council rounds 59–60), registered\n   351\tbefore anything is sent.**\n   352\t- **Request changes** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the v13 request is untouched, its plan hash is unchanged):\n   353\t  - \"If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that\n   354\t    justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap.\" This replaces the former\n   355\t    unresolved sentence.\n   356\t  - \"discounted by default\" becomes \"discounted subject to the evidence requirement below\".\n   357\t  - The qualifier list adds \"estimated\" (cases 111 of 111, mutants 43 of 43, rehearsal 19 of 19).\n   358\t- **Readers.** Shared rule text (`blind_rules.py`), including the allowed qualifier values and the unresolved ruling. First readers'\n   359\t  step 2 asks:\n   360\t  - for a forecast, the evidence for the band-deciding assumption;\n   361\t  - for an unresolved answer, the gap left after the strongest passages.\n   362\t  Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   363\t- **Scope** (`blind4.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`):\n   364\t  - cases S, E and P-v8;\n   365\t  - 6 unedited first replies (a fresh Claude subagent and a fresh read-only Codex session per case);\n   366\t  - 9 blinded packets (6 replies + 3 controls), each read by one Claude and one Codex reader: 18 first readings, plus adjudication\n   367\t    where they disagree.\n   368\t- **Controls**, each checked by the code before use:\n   369\t\n   370\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   371\t  |---|---|---|\n   372\t  | K-unsupported | the saved third-run E Codex reply, with an unsized 15% cut | not supported |\n   373\t  | K-unresolved-unjustified | P-v8 Claude made unresolved with a vague reason | not supported |\n   374\t  | K-unresolved-justified | S made unresolved with the specific gap | supported |\n   375\t- **Pass:**\n   376\t  - every S and E reply has a settled \"yes\" on its forecast or on its reason for leaving the answer unresolved;\n   377\t  - both P-v8 replies have a settled \"yes\" on a forecast;\n   378\t  - all three controls read as expected;\n   379\t  - the refusal and defect stop lines hold as before.\n   380\t- **Otherwise:**\n   381\t  - a support split that adjudication does not settle leaves the run inconclusive, and it goes back to council;\n   382\t  - any failure goes back to council;\n   383\t  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\n   384\t\n   385\t**[D747] The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (Alex: \"可以发布，要讲清楚\"; council rounds 61–61b), registered before any packet is\n   386\tbuilt.**\n   387\t- **The one change.** The shared definitions (`blind_rules.py`, \"supported\") add Alex's ruling: \"When the passages do not give the\n   388\t  asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure only if the answer explains why the comparison applies to the companies,\n   389\t  the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely;\n   390\t  an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling).\"\n   391\t  - The first readers' step 2 asks, for a forecast, whether the passages support the deciding assumption's size or a bound, or whether\n   392\t    the answer explains a related figure as the definitions require.\n   393\t  - Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   394\t  - The requests and the answers are unchanged. Each packet carries its writer's own request.\n   395\t- **What is re-read.** Nine stored answers under a fresh shuffle, W01–W09 (`blind4.py reread`). The key is in `reread-key.json`\n   396\t  beside, never in a packet.\n   397\t\n   398\t  | Answer | Expected reading |\n   399\t  |---|---|\n   400\t  | blind4 U01: P-v8, vague unresolved control | not supported |\n   401\t  | blind4 U03: E, unsized 15% cut control | not supported |\n   402\t  | blind4 U05: E Codex, unsized 10% cut | not supported |\n   403\t  | blind3 T15: E Claude, a bracket between the market pace and the guidance, with Vera as all CPU revenue | supported |\n   404\t  | blind4 U02 (S Claude, 98% share), U08 (S Codex, $25B deduction) and U04 (S justified unresolved); blind3 T10 (S Claude, read for its spending argument only: its settled quotation error stands, and its defect marks are not scored); blind4 U06 (E Claude, 1.8x with a 90% Vera share, judged with its own share) | must settle either way: both first readers agree, or both adjudicators do |\n   405\t- **Readers.** Fresh readers: one Claude subagent and one read-only Codex session per packet. Where they disagree, two adjudicators\n   406\t  read the packet, one per family.\n   407\t- **Scoring** (`blind4.py reread-tally`):\n   408\t  - It passes when the four fixed readings come out as expected and the five others settle.\n   409\t  - Matching votes whose stated reasons contradict each other on the comparison are recorded as unsettled. Claude reads them by hand.\n   410\t  - A settled defect on a writer's reply other than T10 is a stop.\n   411\t- **What the result decides:**\n   412\t  - Pass: the reading standard under D747 is settled. The next step is the E writer repair, with the council before any send, then a\n   413\t    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n   414\t  - A split: Alex's words do not yet fix the reading. The split answers go back to the council, and to Alex as boundary cases.\n   415\t  - A failed fixed reading goes back to the council.\n   416\t  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n     1\t**Ask Alex whether an indirect comparison can justify publishing the more likely forecast. I lean yes. Keep the paid re-send on hold.** The $36B Vera answer shows why these are separate decisions. Its writer acknowledged that the passages gave no order size or shipment pace, then used a $4B shortfall to put sales below $40B. Both first readers rejected that answer. Resolving every disagreement about cloud spending would still leave D745 failed. [Vera answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Agree with Claude’s revised lean. I dispute two of the reasons given for it.** The writer is asked for the most likely outcome. The frozen examples make that concrete. For q19, the price per CPU tells the reader that the target implies four million units in roughly two selling quarters. That supports a discount even though no passage supplies the final $8–18B range. For q04, the reader weighs spending plans, demand and delays. D741 still applies when a writer starts from total spending to forecast a narrower amount. It must state the deduction or give a supported bound. The open choice is whether an indirect comparison can support the stated deduction strongly enough to pick a side. Alex has not already approved that threshold. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented or mislabelled figure. And §8’s “no data to verify” concerns checking outcomes afterward. It gives no permission to publish an unsupported estimate today. [Writer instructions:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:1) · [q19:897](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897) · [q04:246](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [D741:11359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11359) · [Limits of the code checks:11265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265) · [AGENTS.md:416](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:416)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Agree on asking one general question. Disagree with wording that assumes the related number already points to the answer.** The four companies said they expected to spend more than $730B on *AI infrastructure* in 2026. The question asks about *data-center capital spending*. A writer who starts from $745B of total spending has to explain why spending outside data centers is likely to be at most $45B to reach the $700B cutoff. That gives Alex a concrete choice without asking him to define “AI infrastructure” for all future cases. [Spending passages:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:27) · [Two readings of the $45B difference:36](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36)\n     6\t\n     7\t   **My question for Alex:** “资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？”\n     8\t\n     9\t   - **可以发布（我推荐）。** 要讲清这个比较为什么适用于所问的公司、时间和数字，以及按答案自己的假设，为什么这一边更可能。\n    10\t   - **先说‘还判断不了’。** 等资料能直接给出或推算出所问的数在线的哪一边，再发布预测。\n    11\t\n    12\t   This covers using market growth as a comparison for one vendor. It does not make market growth a guaranteed minimum. Both readers accepted the earlier $37B Vera answer, which explained why NVIDIA might outgrow the market. The later answer added a 90% Vera share. At its lower growth estimate, $20B × 1.5 × 90% gives **$27B**. The writer itself acknowledges that sensitivity. The reader who claimed the whole range stayed at $30–40B dropped the writer’s assumption. That error is settled by arithmetic. Whether the writer gave enough reason to prefer its $32.4B forecast was still a split vote. [Earlier Vera reasoning:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [T15 votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later Vera answer:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Adjudicator’s claim:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1)\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Agree with the stored-answer re-read as the one next test.** After Alex rules, keep the answers fixed and change only the shared reading instructions to include his words. The synthetic passage in round 61 would show whether a fresh writer can use an explicit bound. It would tell us little about why two readers looking at the *same* answer disagree. Claude’s list contains **eight distinct answers** because U04 is both a split and a control. I would add T15 as the real comparison for U06. That gives **nine answers, 18 independent first readings, zero new writer calls and $0 in paid sends**. T10 is there for its spending argument only. Its previously confirmed quotation error stands. [Round 61 proposal:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md:32) · [U04 and other controls:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4) · [T10’s separate error:111](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:111)\n    15\t\n    16\t   **What would move the decision:** Before reading, record what Alex’s choice implies for the examples and controls. Move on to writer repair if both families independently apply that choice to the spending forecasts and the “can’t tell” answer, reject the unsupported Vera cuts, and judge T15 and U06 using each writer’s actual assumptions. Matching votes for incompatible reasons do not settle the disagreement. A continuing split leaves the publication question unresolved in practice. Even a clean re-read cannot turn D745 into a pass. [Shared reading rule:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [D741’s sequence:11364](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11364)\n    17\t\n    18\t4. **Agree on waiting for an E repair. Disagree that a paid STOP is already predictable.** Of four Vera answers, **two were rejected by both readers, one split, and one was accepted by both**. Three contained a cut or share whose size is in question. Those four answers came from Codex and Claude. They give no measured failure rate for Luna, and the free readers voted on support while the paid readers separately mark whether a gap decided the band. Paid S also asks about **2027**. The stored S answers concern **2026**. [Earlier Vera votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Paid cases:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:15) · [Free cases:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:169)\n    19\t\n    20\t   **The attainable paid verdict is conditional.** On a complete run, one confirmed deciding gap in any of the three published Vera answers forces **STOP**, even if joint judging wins all nine pairs. Codex breaks a reader tie. With zero published defects across all joint and singleton answers, **PASS** remains possible with **8 joint wins and at most 5 separate wins**, or **9 joint wins and at most 6 separate wins**, with at least two usable joint pairs per snapshot. A properly explained “can’t tell” is published for a singleton and is still inspected. So the 36 calls could measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. The estimated $0.17 cost and proposed $0.50 ceiling do not override the requirement for a clean free test before requesting authorization. [Paid reading and verdict:262](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Verdict calculation:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772) · [Singleton publication:782](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Cost estimate:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:32) · [Authorization condition:380](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:380)\n    21\t\n    22\t   **The smallest writer change I would take forward after the ruling** is to make the writer choose between the existing “forecast” and “can’t tell” answers *before* it assigns a discount. At that choice, it must identify what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. If it can explain only why sales might slip, it can report that gap directly. This is a proposed repair. D742 addressed a different problem involving quarterly guidance. D745 is the round that directly tried and failed to fix unsupported discounts. Automatically publishing management’s number whenever it sits on the cutoff would change the product and would require Alex’s separate decision. [Existing answer choices:287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:287) · [D742:11397](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11397) · [D745:11513](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11513) · [Recurring failures:85](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:85)     1\t**The FAIL is real. Ask Alex what evidence is enough to publish a forecast before requesting the paid re-send.** The decisive case is the $36B Vera forecast. Its writer identified a risk to sales, then assigned that risk a $4B cost. Neither reader found evidence for the amount. That one answer fails the registered test regardless of how the four disagreements are resolved. [E writer’s assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — U05 deserves its settled “no.”** Management expected FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double a $20B base. The writer used $40B as its reference and forecast 90% realization. The deployment announcement supports demand. Anthropic’s evaluation and AMD’s benchmarks establish competitive risk. None gives the timing or volume of sales likely to slip. Calling $4B “less than half an average quarter” expresses the same assumption in another unit. It adds no evidence for it. At exactly $40B the answer enters the higher band, so the unsupported cut decides the result. **Wording correction:** the result report says four readers rejected U05. The record has two first readers, in agreement, and no adjudication. [E request and bands](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) · [Writer’s conclusion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) · [Codex reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex.json:5) · [Claude reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.claude.json:1) · [Report wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:41)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Serious — The four splits have different merits.** I read both initial readings and both adjudications for each. All eight adjudicator votes followed their own family. My readings below leave the recorded splits intact. [Vote tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Reply | My reading | What decides it |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | **U02 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on support. The range is valid.** | The writer applies 98% to $720B and gets $705.6B. Its own 96% lower share gives $691.2B. Crossing $700B is allowed: the checker actually publishes $690.9–720.3B, and the schema permits selecting an overlapping band with a reason. The weakness is the claimed independent floor. If all of the cited **more than $730B** of AI infrastructure really is data-center capex for these firms, it exceeds even the writer’s **total** spending midpoint of $720B. The writer needed to reconcile those estimates before using one to validate the other. [Writer’s reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Checked range](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [Schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/schema.json:334) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.claude.json:1) |\n    10\t   | **U04 · S unresolved control** | **Codex “yes” is better supported.** | At $745B total, the four firms cross $700B at a data-center share of about 94%. The control names that boundary and addresses the strongest contrary passage. The $730B figure names the right four companies but measures AI infrastructure. The nearly $800B data-center figure covers five companies. Neither establishes how much of the four firms’ spending qualifies. Claude has a plausible reason to lean upward. Its claim that the first figure supplies a floor requires a relationship the passage leaves open. That makes this a defensible abstention and a poor control for an *undisputed* abstention. [Control](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [Four-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Five-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.claude.json:1) |\n    11\t   | **U06 · E forecast** | **Codex “no.” The existing definition settles the error in Claude’s justification.** | Keep the writer’s 90% Vera share and run its proposed lower growth rate. **$20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B**, one band below the claimed $30–40B. Claude effectively substitutes a 100% share to obtain $30B. The definition requires judging the answer with the writer’s own assumptions. Claude is right on one factual detail: e4856 does connect Vera to the $20B potential. That passage still supplies neither the writer’s realized share nor a floor for NVIDIA’s growth equal to the whole market’s. The writer’s $32.4B arithmetic is correct. The reader’s claim about the whole interval is wrong. [Writer’s reasoning and sensitivity](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Vera passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:27) · [Definition](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1) |\n    12\t   | **U08 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on the claimed bound. This is the closest call on the forecast itself.** | At $745B total, spending outside data centers can be at most $45B for the forecast to clear $700B. The writer deducts $25B and explicitly allows some AI infrastructure spending outside data centers. Claude’s comparison implies less than $15B **outside AI**. It leaves the amount **outside data centers** open on the writer’s own terms. Meta’s roughly 4% residual offers a concrete comparison, but the writer gives no reason the combined residual of all four firms stays below $45B. Alex’s open question bears directly on whether that comparison nevertheless supports a most-likely forecast. [Writer’s deduction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.claude.json:1) |\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Serious — The recurring writer failure is assigning a size to a risk when that size selects the answer. The recurring reader disagreement is how much a comparison can support.** Counting each original reply once, excluding controls, gives the following. For run three I used its last authorized reading. A joint reply counts once when both forecasts were supported. [Third-run votes by reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:92) · [Third-run usable replies by case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:198) · [Targeted-run votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n    15\t\n    16\t   | Original replies | Settled yes | Settled no | Split |\n    17\t   |---|---:|---:|---:|\n    18\t   | Third run, 12 | 8 | 2 | 2 |\n    19\t   | Targeted run, 6 | 2 | 1 | 3 |\n    20\t   | **Total, 18** | **10** | **3** | **5** |\n    21\t\n    22\t   Across **eight S and E replies**, one had settled support, three had settled rejection, and four split. All eight forecast a number. The clearest recurrence is the E Codex writer cutting the guidance reference by 15% in run three and 10% after the request explicitly asked for evidence sizing the cut. All eight P-family replies across the runs had settled support. O accounts for the remaining yes and split, on whether $18B described funding of an existing commitment balance. The stored votes therefore establish three rejected replies, not eight writer errors. They also show reader variation: the *same* third-run S Codex answer went **no → yes → no** across its three readings. [Earlier E cut](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T17.codex.json:5) · [Revised request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:9) · [O disagreement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T02.codex.json:5) · [S original vote](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:494) · [First re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:291) · [Second re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:123)\n    23\t\n    24\t4. **Serious — Alex settled the duty to justify an adjustment. How strong an indirect comparison must be is still his decision.** D741 requires the deduction to be stated or a supported bound given. D745 accepts an unresolved answer when its reason survives the strongest passages. The writer is also told to choose the most likely outcome and to size estimates from comparable deals, capacity, units, selling time, or price. The frozen examples illustrate the distinction. On q04, the reader used roughly $650B of plans and evidence about demand, costs, and delays to forecast $650–720B. On q19, two selling quarters and a $5,000 CPU price made the four million units implied by guidance assessable. No passage supplied the final discount. Those examples support reasoned estimates. They neither establish S’s category relationship nor provide the missing sales evidence for FY2028 Vera. [D741](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348) · [D745](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11510) · [Writer’s sizing instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:4) · [q04 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [q19 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897)\n    25\t\n    26\t5. **Minor — Two details change how to interpret the paid option.** Its third reader is Codex. A published singleton stops the test if a reader-confirmed unsized adjustment decides its band. The free test asked about *support*, so these votes indicate a risk, not a measured paid-stop rate. Also, paid S asks about **2027** while targeted S asks about **2026**. Finally, the report’s statement that an unresolved singleton is automatically held is inaccurate. I replayed all nine stored answers and controls in memory. Both unresolved controls returned `publish` with no numeric outcome. Such a card has no chosen band, but the paid verdict does inspect published singleton cards for defects. [Paid reading and stop rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Paid cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:43) · [Targeted cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:166) · [Singleton publication rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Paid verdict code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772)\n    27\t\n    28\t**One recommendation.** Put this question to Alex: **“When a related figure makes one band more likely, but the evidence still leaves the next band plausible, may we publish the forecast if we explain why the comparison applies, or should we say ‘unresolved’ until the evidence bounds the quantity on the deciding side?”** I favor allowing the forecast when the writer explains why the comparison applies to the companies, period, and quantity asked about, and why it favors that side of the cutoff. U05 fails even that reading.\n    29\t\n    30\tThat ruling costs no provider spend. A fresh read under unchanged instructions would sample the same disagreement. A code or request change now would spend engineering and review time implementing one side before Alex chooses it. Closing S and E wholesale costs both forecasts whenever the evidence later becomes sufficient. The 36-call paid re-send would measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. It cannot settle what Alex wants published. The earlier plan estimated **$0.17** for first attempts at typical output length under a **$0.50** ceiling; a new send requires separate authorization. [Registered sequence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375) · [Paid cost assumptions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:29)\n    31\t\n    32\t**One test.** After Alex rules, register one **$0 blinded contrast**. Give fresh writers the same spending question twice. One version has only the indirect comparison Alex ruled on. The other adds a clearly labeled test passage that explicitly bounds the four firms’ data-center spending above $700B and below $800B. Fix the expected decisions from Alex’s answer before showing either version. Mix in the saved U05 as the unsupported-cut control. The result moves the decision only if both reader families apply Alex’s rule to the indirect case, accept a forecast on the explicitly bounded case, and reject U05’s unsized cut. Agreement would justify returning to writer verification under a settled standard. Another split would keep the paid request closed. The original D745 FAIL stands either way. 11346\t## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\n 11347\t\n 11348\t- **The questions** (after D740), each with its options; the recommended option is listed first:\n 11349\t  - 【增速参考】\"写手用公司指引的三季度 1080 亿算出“指引隐含增速约 12%”，参考它定四季度增速 13%，再套在自己预测的三季度 1136 亿上。进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个。这算不算“同一季度用了两个数”的错？\" Options: 不算错 (推荐); 算错.\n 11350\t  - 【市场参照】\"分析师预期公司全年总收入 4110 亿，最近一个季度数据中心占 92.5%，乘出来约 3800 亿。能不能把这 3800 亿叫作“市场对数据中心全年的预期”？\" Options: 不能 (推荐); 能.\n 11351\t  - 【总开支当数据中心开支】\"问题问四大云厂商“数据中心资本开支”，原文只给了“总资本开支”（并说大部分投向 AI 基础设施）。一份答案直接拿总开支当数据中心开支，没扣非数据中心的部分。可以吗？\" Options: 必须写明扣多少 (推荐); 可以直接用.\n 11352\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"不算错 (推荐)\"; \"不能 (推荐)\"; \"必须写明扣多少 (推荐)\".\n 11353\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11354\t  1. **Not a defect:** a growth rate computed from guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate, when one figure\n 11355\t     supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation.\n 11356\t  2. **Not the market's reference:** consensus for a total × a past quarter's reported share. That figure is our comparison estimate.\n 11357\t     Only an outside figure for the member's own quantity (for example Morningstar's direct segment estimate), or a calculation that\n 11358\t     carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period, is the market's reference.\n 11359\t  3. **A total used for a narrower quantity** (total capex for data-center capex) must state the deduction as its own figure, or give\n 11360\t     a supported bound that shows where the narrower quantity lands.\n 11361\t- **How they are applied (D738):**\n 11362\t  - Ruling 2's carried share is structural, so S5 refuses it.\n 11363\t  - Rulings 1 and 3 go in the request and in the reader brief, because they turn on what a label or an estimate means.\n 11364\t  - Then three steps:\n 11365\t    1. fix the S1 \"from … to …\" range (D740);\n 11366\t    2. check that both reader families apply the rulings the same way to the stored boundary replies;\n 11367\t    3. re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers.\n 11368\t\n 11369\t## D742 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s re-run stops on one sound answer refused by S4; council 56–56c keeps S4 and makes the request state one benchmark rule; a third $0 blind run follows\n 11370\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '385,416p'\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py | sed -n '285,345p'\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '385,416p'\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py | sed -n '285,345p'\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py\"","aggregated_output":"   385\t**[D747] The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (Alex: \"可以发布，要讲清楚\"; council rounds 61–61b), registered before any packet is\n   386\tbuilt.**\n   387\t- **The one change.** The shared definitions (`blind_rules.py`, \"supported\") add Alex's ruling: \"When the passages do not give the\n   388\t  asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure only if the answer explains why the comparison applies to the companies,\n   389\t  the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely;\n   390\t  an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling).\"\n   391\t  - The first readers' step 2 asks, for a forecast, whether the passages support the deciding assumption's size or a bound, or whether\n   392\t    the answer explains a related figure as the definitions require.\n   393\t  - Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   394\t  - The requests and the answers are unchanged. Each packet carries its writer's own request.\n   395\t- **What is re-read.** Nine stored answers under a fresh shuffle, W01–W09 (`blind4.py reread`). The key is in `reread-key.json`\n   396\t  beside, never in a packet.\n   397\t\n   398\t  | Answer | Expected reading |\n   399\t  |---|---|\n   400\t  | blind4 U01: P-v8, vague unresolved control | not supported |\n   401\t  | blind4 U03: E, unsized 15% cut control | not supported |\n   402\t  | blind4 U05: E Codex, unsized 10% cut | not supported |\n   403\t  | blind3 T15: E Claude, a bracket between the market pace and the guidance, with Vera as all CPU revenue | supported |\n   404\t  | blind4 U02 (S Claude, 98% share), U08 (S Codex, $25B deduction) and U04 (S justified unresolved); blind3 T10 (S Claude, read for its spending argument only: its settled quotation error stands, and its defect marks are not scored); blind4 U06 (E Claude, 1.8x with a 90% Vera share, judged with its own share) | must settle either way: both first readers agree, or both adjudicators do |\n   405\t- **Readers.** Fresh readers: one Claude subagent and one read-only Codex session per packet. Where they disagree, two adjudicators\n   406\t  read the packet, one per family.\n   407\t- **Scoring** (`blind4.py reread-tally`):\n   408\t  - It passes when the four fixed readings come out as expected and the five others settle.\n   409\t  - Matching votes whose stated reasons contradict each other on the comparison are recorded as unsettled. Claude reads them by hand.\n   410\t  - A settled defect on a writer's reply other than T10 is a stop.\n   411\t- **What the result decides:**\n   412\t  - Pass: the reading standard under D747 is settled. The next step is the E writer repair, with the council before any send, then a\n   413\t    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n   414\t  - A split: Alex's words do not yet fix the reading. The split answers go back to the council, and to Alex as boundary cases.\n   415\t  - A failed fixed reading goes back to the council.\n   416\t  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n     1\t\"\"\"The one source of the defect definitions and the writer's format material for judging-v13s blind readers and adjudicators (D744).\n     2\t\n     3\tReaders and adjudicators get the same text: the writer's system text, every schema description, and these definitions, so that what\n     4\tthe format rules permit is never marked as a defect by one and cleared by the other.\n     5\t\"\"\"\n     6\timport json\n     7\timport sys\n     8\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     9\t\n    10\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    11\tfrom calc_small import QUALIFIERS  # noqa: E402\n    12\t\n    13\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    14\tnot a defect):\n    15\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    16\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages (its size, or a bound that justifies the rung).\n    17\t  When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure only if the answer explains why the\n    18\t  comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated assumptions, the\n    19\t  chosen side of the line is more likely; an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling). For an unresolved\n    20\t  member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung unsettled (a\n    21\t  ruling).\n    22\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    23\t  format rules permit.\n    24\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    25\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    26\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    27\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    28\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    29\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    30\t  quarter.\n    31\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    32\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    33\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    34\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    35\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    36\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    37\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    38\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef descriptions(schema, path=''):\n    42\t    \"\"\"Every description in the schema, keyed by its field path.\"\"\"\n    43\t    found = {}\n    44\t    if isinstance(schema, dict):\n    45\t        if isinstance(schema.get('description'), str):\n    46\t            found[path.strip('/') or 'answer'] = schema['description']\n    47\t        for key, value in schema.items():\n    48\t            if key != 'description':\n    49\t                step = '' if key in ('properties', 'items', 'anyOf') else key\n    50\t                found.update(descriptions(value, f'{path}/{step}' if step else path))\n    51\t    elif isinstance(schema, list):\n    52\t        for value in schema:\n    53\t            found.update(descriptions(value, path))\n    54\t    return found\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\tdef format_material(case_folder):\n    58\t    \"\"\"The writer's system text and every schema description, as shown to readers and adjudicators.\"\"\"\n    59\t    case_folder = Path(case_folder)\n    60\t    schema = json.loads((case_folder / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    61\t    return '\\n'.join(['# The system text the writer was given', (case_folder / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    62\t                      '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    63\t                      json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    64\t                      'The qualifier values the format allows (a qualifier not in this list cannot be recorded): '\n    65\t                      + ', '.join(QUALIFIERS) + '.'])\n   285\tdef reread_tally(out):\n   286\t    \"\"\"[D747] Registered lines: the four fixed readings as expected; every other answer settled (either way); a settled defect on a\n   287\t    writer's reply is a stop, except T10's marks (its quotation error is already settled, D744).\"\"\"\n   288\t    key = json.loads((out / 'reread-key.json').read_text())\n   289\t    lines, need, verdict, defects = [], [], 'pass', []\n   290\t    for pid, item in sorted(key.items()):\n   291\t        first = [_reading(out / 'reread-packets' / f'{pid}.{w}.json') for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   292\t        adjudicated = [out / 'reread-adjudication' / f'{pid}.{w}.json' for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   293\t        adj = [_reading(p) for p in adjudicated] if all(p.exists() for p in adjudicated) else None\n   294\t        answer = json.loads(Path(item['answer']).read_text())\n   295\t        marks = [{_defect_key(d) for d in r.get('defects', [])} for r in first]\n   296\t        either = (marks[0] | marks[1]) - (marks[0] & marks[1])\n   297\t        for d in marks[0] & marks[1]:\n   298\t            defects.append([pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), 'both readers'])\n   299\t        for a in answer['answers']:\n   300\t            view = a['view_id']\n   301\t            votes = [r['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for r in first]\n   302\t            if votes[0] == votes[1]:\n   303\t                value = votes[0]\n   304\t            elif adj is None:\n   305\t                need.append(pid)\n   306\t                continue\n   307\t            else:\n   308\t                second = [x['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported') for x in adj]\n   309\t                votes += second\n   310\t                value = second[0] if second[0] == second[1] else 'split'\n   311\t            expected = REREAD_EXPECTED.get(item['label'])\n   312\t            ok = value == expected if expected else value in ('yes', 'no')\n   313\t            if not ok:\n   314\t                verdict = 'fail' if expected and value != 'split' else ('inconclusive' if verdict == 'pass' else verdict)\n   315\t            lines.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]:11} {item[\"case\"]:5} {item[\"writer\"]:25} {view} {a[\"shape\"]:10} settled={value:6} '\n   316\t                         f'votes={votes} [{\"expects \" + expected if expected else \"must settle\"}] {\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   317\t        if either and adj is None and pid not in need:\n   318\t            need.append(pid)\n   319\t        elif either and adj is not None:\n   320\t            decisions = [{_defect_key(d): d.get('decision') for d in x.get('defects', [])} for x in adj]\n   321\t            for d in sorted(either, key=str):\n   322\t                pair = [decisions[0].get(d), decisions[1].get(d)]\n   323\t                if pair != ['reject', 'reject']:\n   324\t                    defects.append([pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), pair])\n   325\t    if need:\n   326\t        print('adjudication needed for:', ' '.join(sorted(set(need))))\n   327\t        return\n   328\t    stops = [d for d in defects if (d[4] == 'both readers' or d[4] == ['uphold', 'uphold']) and not d[1].startswith('blind3 T10')\n   329\t             and 'K-' not in key[d[0]]['writer']]\n   330\t    if stops:\n   331\t        verdict = 'fail'\n   332\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'lines': lines, 'defects': defects, 'stops': stops}\n   333\t    (out / 'REREAD-TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   334\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n   335\t    print('defects (settled or split):', defects)\n   336\t    print('stops:', stops)\n   337\t    print('VERDICT:', verdict)\n   338\t\n   339\t\n   340\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   341\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) == 3 else ''\n   342\t    target = Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve() if len(sys.argv) == 3 else None\n   343\t    {'build': build, 'check': check, 'packets': packets, 'tally': tally, 'reread': reread, 'reread-tally': reread_tally}.get(\n   344\t        cmd, lambda _: (_ for _ in ()).throw(SystemExit(__doc__)))(target)\n     1\t\"\"\"Adjudication and refusal-review packets for a judging-v13s blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5, D742).\n     2\t\n     3\tAdjudication: for every reply whose two readers disagree (a member's \"supported\", or any defect one reader marks), one packet holding\n     4\tthe two readings, the system text, the format rules the writer was given (every schema description), the request as the writer saw it\n     5\tand the answer. Reader 1 is the Codex reading and Reader 2 the Claude reading; the adjudicators are not told which.\n     6\t\n     7\tRefusal review: for every refused first reply, one packet with the refusal messages, the system text, the schema, the user text and the\n     8\tanswer, asking the two registered judgements apart: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound, and does the exact request\n     9\tclearly state the condition the refusal enforces (D742: a sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run).\n    10\t\n    11\tUsage: python3 blind_adjudicate.py <blind test folder> <packet id> [<packet id> ...]\n    12\t       python3 blind_adjudicate.py refusal <blind test folder> <case> <writer>\n    13\t\"\"\"\n    14\timport json\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    19\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, descriptions, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    20\t\n    21\tHEADER = \"\"\"# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n    22\t\n    23\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n    24\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n    25\t\n    26\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no). For each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the\n    27\tpassage words or the format rule that decides it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    30\t\n    31\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    32\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    33\t\"\"\"\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef _json(text):\n    37\t    \"\"\"A reading: the outermost JSON object in the file.\"\"\"\n    38\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef reading_lines(label, reading):\n    42\t    lines = []\n    43\t    for member, mark in sorted(reading.get('members', {}).items()):\n    44\t        line = f\"- {label} on member {member}: supported = {mark.get('supported')}. Ground: {mark.get('ground', '')}\"\n    45\t        if mark.get('why_not'):\n    46\t            line += f\" Why not: {mark['why_not']}\"\n    47\t        lines.append(line)\n    48\t    defects = reading.get('defects', [])\n    49\t    if not defects:\n    50\t        lines.append(f'- {label} marks no defect.')\n    51\t    for defect in defects:\n    52\t        lines.append(f\"- {label} marks a defect: {defect.get('type')} (figures {defect.get('figures', [])}): {defect.get('detail', '')}\")\n    53\t    return lines\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\tdef build(folder, packet_ids, key_name='reader-key.json', packets='reader-packets', out_name='adjudication'):\n    57\t    key = json.loads((folder / key_name).read_text())\n    58\t    out = folder / out_name\n    59\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    60\t    for pid in packet_ids:\n    61\t        case, writer = key[pid]['case'], key[pid]['writer']\n    62\t        codex = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.codex.json').read_text())\n    63\t        claude = _json((folder / packets / f'{pid}.claude.json').read_text())\n    64\t        answer_path = folder / key[pid]['answer'] if key[pid].get('answer') else folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'\n    65\t        case_dir = Path(key[pid]['case_dir']) if key[pid].get('case_dir') else folder / case   # D747: the writer's own request\n    66\t        text = '\\n'.join([HEADER, '# The two readings', *reading_lines('Reader 1', codex), *reading_lines('Reader 2', claude), '',\n    67\t                          format_material(case_dir), '',\n    68\t                          '# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)', (case_dir / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n    69\t                          '# The answer', answer_path.read_text()])\n    70\t        target = out / f'{pid}.md'\n    71\t        if target.exists():\n    72\t            raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n    73\t        target.write_text(text)\n    74\t        (out / f'{pid}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n    75\t                                               'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n    76\t        print(f'{pid}: {case}/{writer}, {len(text)} chars')\n    77\t\n    78\t\n    79\tREFUSAL_HEADER = \"\"\"# Refusal review (judging-v13s blind test)\n    80\t\n    81\tA code check refused the answer below. For each refusal message, give two judgements apart, from the request text and the passages\n    82\talone:\n    83\t1. sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound against the passages (would an analyst accept the number and the reasons\n    84\t   that produce it)? Judge this on its own, apart from the format rule the check enforces. Answer yes or no, with why.\n    85\t2. stated: does the exact request (system text, schema or user text below) clearly state the condition the refusal enforces? Answer\n    86\t   yes or no, and quote the sentence that states it, or say none does.\n    87\tReturn only JSON: {\"refusals\": [{\"message\": \"...\", \"sound\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\", \"stated\": \"yes|no\", \"sentence\": \"...\"}]}\n    88\t\"\"\"\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\tdef refusal(folder, case, writer):\n    92\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((folder / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n    93\t    row = checked[(case, writer)]\n    94\t    if row.get('decision') == 'publish' or not row.get('errors'):\n    95\t        raise SystemExit(f'{case}/{writer} was not refused')\n    96\t    out = folder / 'refusal-review'\n    97\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    98\t    text = '\\n'.join([REFUSAL_HEADER, '# Refusal messages', json.dumps(row['errors'], ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    99\t                      '# The system text', (folder / case / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n   100\t                      '# The schema', (folder / case / 'schema.json').read_text(), '',\n   101\t                      '# The user text (the request, with every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n   102\t                      '# The answer', (folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json').read_text()])\n   103\t    target = out / f'{case}-{writer}.md'\n   104\t    if target.exists():\n   105\t        raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n   106\t    target.write_text(text)\n   107\t    (out / f'{case}-{writer}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n   108\t                                                    'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n   109\t    print(f'refusal review {case}/{writer}: {len(text)} chars')\n   110\t\n   111\t\n   112\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   113\t    if len(sys.argv) == 5 and sys.argv[1] == 'refusal':\n   114\t        refusal(Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve(), sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4])\n   115\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread':        # D743: the re-read's packets, key and adjudication folder\n   116\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   117\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread2':       # D744: the second re-read under the shared rule text\n   118\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread2-key.json', 'reread2-packets', 'reread2-adjudication')\n   119\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 4 and sys.argv[2] == '--reread-d747':   # D747: the re-read under Alex's comparison ruling\n   120\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[3:], 'reread-key.json', 'reread-packets', 'reread-adjudication')\n   121\t    elif len(sys.argv) >= 3 and sys.argv[1] != 'refusal':\n   122\t        build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[2:])\n   123\t    else:\n   124\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | head -92\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread2.py | head -85\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61b.md | cut -c1-850\nrg -n 'QUALIFIERS' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json\nwc -lc poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/{E,S,P-v8}/{user.txt,system.txt,answers/*.json}\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | head -92\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_reread2.py | head -85\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61b.md | cut -c1-850\nrg -n 'QUALIFIERS' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json\nwc -lc poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/{E,S,P-v8}/{user.txt,system.txt,answers/*.json}\"","aggregated_output":"# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n     1\t\"\"\"The D744 second blinded re-read of a v13s blind run (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D744]): the same first replies, read with the same rule text\n     2\tadjudicators get (blind_rules: system text, every schema description, the definitions), plus five controls built from fresh copies:\n     3\tK-midpoint and K-misread (P's Claude reply, its \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance as the midpoint 108 or as 108.5), K-overlap and K-disjoint\n     4\t(E's Claude reply with a growth range giving $36-43B, rung 3 with its reason or rung 4, text made consistent), and K-unsupported (E's\n     5\tClaude reply with a 140% growth estimate its own reasons argue against). Each control is checked by the code before it is used.\n     6\t\n     7\tUsage: python3 blind_reread2.py <blind run folder>\n     8\t\"\"\"\n     9\timport copy\n    10\timport hashlib\n    11\timport json\n    12\timport random\n    13\timport sys\n    14\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    15\t\n    16\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    17\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parents[2] / 'src'))\n    19\timport calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\n    20\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    21\t\n    22\tREADER_BRIEF = \"\"\"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, second re-read, D744)\n    23\t\n    24\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n    25\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n    26\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n    27\t\n    28\tJudge in two steps:\n    29\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n    30\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    31\t2. For each member, name the evidence for the assumption that puts the forecast in its band, and say whether the passages support it.\n    32\t\n    33\tReturn JSON:\n    34\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    35\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    36\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    37\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    38\t\n    39\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    40\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    41\t\"\"\"\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\tdef _fig(answer, fid):\n    45\t    return next(f for f in answer['figures'] if f['id'] == fid)\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef _replace(text, old, new):\n    49\t    if text.count(old) != 1:\n    50\t        raise SystemExit(f'control text: \"{old[:60]}\" occurs {text.count(old)} times')\n    51\t    return text.replace(old, new)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef controls(out):\n    55\t    made = {}\n    56\t    p = json.loads((out / 'P' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    57\t    guide = next(f for f in p['figures'] if f.get('quote_number') == '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%')\n    58\t    for name, value in (('K-midpoint', 108.0), ('K-misread', 108.5)):\n    59\t        a = copy.deepcopy(p)\n    60\t        _fig(a, guide['id']).update(value=value, low=None, high=None)\n    61\t        made[name] = ('P', a, guide['id'])\n    62\t    e = json.loads((out / 'E' / 'answers' / 'claude.json').read_text())\n    63\t    old_reason = 'I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line.'\n    64\t    for name, rung, reason in (\n    65\t            ('K-overlap', 3, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8). Its midpoint, '\n    66\t             'near $39.5 billion, and most of the range lie in rung 3 (Neutral), so I choose rung 3; the top of the range reaches rung 2.'),\n    67\t            ('K-disjoint', 4, 'I forecast 80% to 115% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $36 billion to $43 billion (F8): rung 4.')):\n    68\t        a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    69\t        f7, f8 = _fig(a, 'F7'), _fig(a, 'F8')\n    70\t        f7.update(value=None, low=80.0, high=115.0, basis_text=_replace(f7['basis_text'], 'gives 85%.', 'gives 80% to 115%.'))\n    71\t        f8.update(value=None, low=36.0, high=43.0)\n    72\t        a['answers'][0].update(rung=rung, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason, reason))\n    73\t        made[name] = ('E', a, 'F8')\n    74\t    a = copy.deepcopy(e)\n    75\t    _fig(a, 'F7').update(value=140.0, basis_text='My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth: 140%, because AI demand is strong.',\n    76\t                         rests_on=['F2'])\n    77\t    _fig(a, 'F8').update(value=48.0)\n    78\t    a['answers'][0].update(rung=2, reasons=_replace(a['answers'][0]['reasons'], old_reason,\n    79\t                                                    'I forecast 140% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $48 billion (F8): rung 2 (Bullish).'))\n    80\t    made['K-unsupported'] = ('E', a, 'F7')\n    81\t    expected = {'K-midpoint': [], 'K-misread': ['S1'], 'K-overlap': [], 'K-disjoint': ['S6'], 'K-unsupported': []}\n    82\t    for name, (case, answer, _) in made.items():\n    83\t        it = json.loads((out / case / 'item.json').read_text())\n    84\t        codes = sorted({c for c, _ in cs.check(it['family'], it['passages'], cs.from_answer(answer, it['family']))['errors']})\n    85\t        if codes != expected[name]:\n     1\t**Ask Alex whether an indirect comparison can justify publishing the more likely forecast. I lean yes. Keep the paid re-send on hold.** The $36B Vera answer shows why these are separate decisions. Its writer acknowledged that the passages gave no order size or shipment pace, then used a $4B shortfall to put sales below $40B. Both first readers rejected that answer. Resolving every disagreement about cloud spending would still leave D745 failed. [Vera answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Agree with Claude’s revised lean. I dispute two of the reasons given for it.** The writer is asked for the most likely outcome. The frozen examples make that concrete. For q19, the price per CPU tells the reader that the target implies four million units in roughly two selling quarters. That supports a discount even though no passage supplies the final $8–18B range. For q04, the reader weighs spending plans, demand and delays. D741 still applies when a writer starts from total spending to forecast a narrower amount. It must state the deduction or give a supported bound. The open choice is whether an indirect comparison can support the stated deduction strongly enough to pick a side. Alex has not already approved that threshold. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented or mislabelled figure. And §8’s “no data to v\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Agree on asking one general question. Disagree with wording that assumes the related number already points to the answer.** The four companies said they expected to spend more than $730B on *AI infrastructure* in 2026. The question asks about *data-center capital spending*. A writer who starts from $745B of total spending has to explain why spending outside data centers is likely to be at most $45B to reach the $700B cutoff. That gives Alex a concrete choice without asking him to define “AI infrastructure” for all future cases. [Spending passages:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:27) · [Two readings of the $45B difference:36](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36)\n     6\t\n     7\t   **My question for Alex:** “资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？”\n     8\t\n     9\t   - **可以发布（我推荐）。** 要讲清这个比较为什么适用于所问的公司、时间和数字，以及按答案自己的假设，为什么这一边更可能。\n    10\t   - **先说‘还判断不了’。** 等资料能直接给出或推算出所问的数在线的哪一边，再发布预测。\n    11\t\n    12\t   This covers using market growth as a comparison for one vendor. It does not make market growth a guaranteed minimum. Both readers accepted the earlier $37B Vera answer, which explained why NVIDIA might outgrow the market. The later answer added a 90% Vera share. At its lower growth estimate, $20B × 1.5 × 90% gives **$27B**. The writer itself acknowledges that sensitivity. The reader who claimed the whole range stayed at $30–40B dropped the writer’s assumption. That error is settled by arithmetic. Whether the writer gave enough reason to prefer its $32.4B forecast was still a split vote. [Earlier Vera reasoning:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [T15 votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later Vera answ\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Agree with the stored-answer re-read as the one next test.** After Alex rules, keep the answers fixed and change only the shared reading instructions to include his words. The synthetic passage in round 61 would show whether a fresh writer can use an explicit bound. It would tell us little about why two readers looking at the *same* answer disagree. Claude’s list contains **eight distinct answers** because U04 is both a split and a control. I would add T15 as the real comparison for U06. That gives **nine answers, 18 independent first readings, zero new writer calls and $0 in paid sends**. T10 is there for its spending argument only. Its previously confirmed quotation error stands. [Round 61 proposal:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md:32) · [U04 and other controls:4](/Users/lching\n    15\t\n    16\t   **What would move the decision:** Before reading, record what Alex’s choice implies for the examples and controls. Move on to writer repair if both families independently apply that choice to the spending forecasts and the “can’t tell” answer, reject the unsupported Vera cuts, and judge T15 and U06 using each writer’s actual assumptions. Matching votes for incompatible reasons do not settle the disagreement. A continuing split leaves the publication question unresolved in practice. Even a clean re-read cannot turn D745 into a pass. [Shared reading rule:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [D741’s sequence:11364](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11364)\n    17\t\n    18\t4. **Agree on waiting for an E repair. Disagree that a paid STOP is already predictable.** Of four Vera answers, **two were rejected by both readers, one split, and one was accepted by both**. Three contained a cut or share whose size is in question. Those four answers came from Codex and Claude. They give no measured failure rate for Luna, and the free readers voted on support while the paid readers separately mark whether a gap decided the band. Paid S also asks about **2027**. The stored S answers concern **2026**. [Earlier Vera votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Paid cases:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:15) · [Free \n    19\t\n    20\t   **The attainable paid verdict is conditional.** On a complete run, one confirmed deciding gap in any of the three published Vera answers forces **STOP**, even if joint judging wins all nine pairs. Codex breaks a reader tie. With zero published defects across all joint and singleton answers, **PASS** remains possible with **8 joint wins and at most 5 separate wins**, or **9 joint wins and at most 6 separate wins**, with at least two usable joint pairs per snapshot. A properly explained “can’t tell” is published for a singleton and is still inspected. So the 36 calls could measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. The estimated $0.17 cost and proposed $0.50 ceiling do not override the requirement for a clean free test before requesting authorization. [Paid reading and verdict:262](/Users/lchingen/hivemind\n    21\t\n    22\t   **The smallest writer change I would take forward after the ruling** is to make the writer choose between the existing “forecast” and “can’t tell” answers *before* it assigns a discount. At that choice, it must identify what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. If it can explain only why sales might slip, it can report that gap directly. This is a proposed repair. D742 addressed a different problem involving quarterly guidance. D745 is the round that directly tried and failed to fix unsupported discounts. Automatically publishing management’s number whenever it sits on the cutoff would change the product and would require Alex’s separate decision. [Existing answer choices:287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:287) · [D742:11397](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISION\n19:QUALIFIERS_BEFORE = sorted(['more than', 'at least', 'up to', 'as much as', 'less than', 'over', 'about', 'approximately', 'around',\n21:QUALIFIERS_AFTER = ['or more', 'or less']\n22:QUALIFIERS = ['none'] + QUALIFIERS_BEFORE + QUALIFIERS_AFTER\n491:            found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_BEFORE if re.search(r'(?:^|\\W)' + re.escape(w) + r'\\s*$', before, re.I)), None)\n493:                found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_AFTER if re.match(r'^\\s*,?\\s*' + re.escape(w) + r'\\b', after, re.I)), None)\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"U01\": {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-unjustified\",\n     5\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json\"\n     6\t },\n     7\t \"U02\": {\n     8\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n     9\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    10\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/claude.json\"\n    11\t },\n    12\t \"U03\": {\n    13\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    14\t  \"writer\": \"K-unsupported\",\n    15\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unsupported.json\"\n    16\t },\n    17\t \"U04\": {\n    18\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    19\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-justified\",\n    20\t  \"answer\": \"controls/K-unresolved-justified.json\"\n    21\t },\n    22\t \"U05\": {\n    23\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    24\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    25\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/codex.json\"\n    26\t },\n    27\t \"U06\": {\n    28\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    29\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    30\t  \"answer\": \"E/answers/claude.json\"\n    31\t },\n    32\t \"U07\": {\n    33\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    34\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    35\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/codex.json\"\n    36\t },\n    37\t \"U08\": {\n    38\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    39\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    40\t  \"answer\": \"S/answers/codex.json\"\n    41\t },\n    42\t \"U09\": {\n    43\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    44\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    45\t  \"answer\": \"P-v8/answers/claude.json\"\n    46\t }\n    47\t}      59   13109 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt\n       9    4367 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/system.txt\n     188    8447 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n     203    6544 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json\n     133   25726 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt\n       9    4367 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/system.txt\n     320   13428 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json\n     277    9919 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json\n     771  149799 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/user.txt\n       9    4367 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/system.txt\n     481   18168 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/claude.json\n     185   11528 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/codex.json\n      59   13109 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n       9    4540 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt\n     188    9740 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n     170    4951 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n     133   25726 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt\n       9    4540 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/system.txt\n     437   17332 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\n     279   10287 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\n     771  149799 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/user.txt\n       9    4540 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/system.txt\n     348   14763 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json\n     348   12540 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json\n    5404  541636 total\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt'","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt | rg '1\\\\s+|2\\\\s+|3\\\\s+|4\\\\s+|730 billion|730B|760 billion|745 billion|720 billion|96%|nearly \\\\\"'$800|750 billion'\"' | cut -c1-1250\nrg -n 'D747' docs/{CURRENT_STATE,DECISIONS}.md | tail -8\ntail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt | rg '1\\\\s+|2\\\\s+|3\\\\s+|4\\\\s+|730 billion|730B|760 billion|745 billion|720 billion|96%|nearly \\\\\"'$800|750 billion'\"' | cut -c1-1250\nrg -n 'D747' docs/{CURRENT_STATE,DECISIONS}.md | tail -8\ntail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n     4\t\n    10\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n    11\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n    12\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    13\t    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n    14\t- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    15\t    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    17\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n    18\t    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n    21\t- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n    22\t    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n    23\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    24\t- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    33\t    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n    34\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    35\t- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n    37\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    38\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n    39\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    40\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    41\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    42\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n    43\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    48\t    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n    50\t- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    51\t    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    52\t- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n    53\t    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n    54\t- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n    57\t- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    59\t- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\n    61\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    62\t- e4564 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-09; from gnews:33edf25ec336133783ceead0 2026-09-09] The article expects top hyperscalers to spend $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 billion in calendar 2027.\n    63\t    Quote: The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\n    64\t- e4566 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Elon Musk said a global memory shortage was the limiting factor for the AI build-out.\n    71\t- e4576 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] Michael Burry says AI chips should be expensed over two to three years, rather than over a longer useful life.\n    72\t    Quote: Burry believes the lifespan of these chips should be shorter, saying they should be expensed over two to three years.\n    73\t- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n    74\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n    75\t- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    76\t    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n    81\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    89\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    91\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    92\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    93\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n    94\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   104\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   105\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   107\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   108\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   110\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   111\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   114\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   117\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   119\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   121\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   122\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   123\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   124\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1090:| D746 | 2026-11-23 | D745 targeted test FAILS: E's Codex reply settled not supported (an unsized 10% cut decides the band); S's two replies, E's Claude reply and the justified-unresolved control split along model family (8 of 8 adjudicator votes); P-v8 2 of 2; 0 of 6 withheld; council 61–61b: ask Alex one general question (may a forecast rest on an explained indirect comparison, or must it wait until the passages settle the deciding side; lean: allow it, explained), then one $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words; the paid re-send stays on hold until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test; question answered in D747 | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1091:| D747 | 2026-11-23 | Alex: \"可以发布，要讲清楚\" — a forecast may rest on a related figure when the passages do not give the asked quantity, supported only when the answer explains why the comparison applies to the companies, period and quantity asked and why, under its own stated assumptions, that side of the line is more likely; D741 ruling 3 and D745's unresolved ruling still hold; next: the words into the shared reader definitions, then one $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers after council review | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:448:**The one next task: the $0 blinded re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (D747), after council round 62 reviews it.** Alex ruled\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:449:(D747, \"可以发布，要讲清楚\") that a forecast may rest on a related figure when the passages do not give the asked quantity. It is\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:454:- The re-read is registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D747]: nine stored answers, four fixed readings and five that must settle.\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:457:The record of the D746 step (Alex answered its question in D747):\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:459:**Earlier next task (done, D747): Alex answers one general question (D746).** \"When the passages do not give the asked number, and\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/{E,S}/answers/{claude,codex}.json; do echo \"$f\"; rg -n '\"'basis_text|reasons|why\\\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | rg -v '\"'null|\\\"\\\"'; done\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/{E,S}/answers/{claude,codex}.json; do echo \"$f\"; rg -n '\"'basis_text|reasons|why\\\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | rg -v '\"'null|\\\"\\\"'; done\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n131:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%.\",\n162:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json\n94:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that Vera realizes most of the stated CPU opportunity in its initial ramp. Expected deployment across major customers supports a high share, while the evidence gives no sales volumes to justify assigning the entire CPU opportunity to realized Vera revenue.\",\n142:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that Vera revenue doubles from the discounted launch-year base as deployments broaden. Management's expansion outlook sets the benchmark for the pace. Anthropic is evaluating Vera, and AMD's platform and agentic-workload benchmarks suggest competition for additional deployments.\",\n178:      \"reasons\": \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation is a concrete sign of interest. Neither statement gives committed purchase volumes. AMD's own tests suggest that Vera faces competition for CPU-intensive workloads, with an uncertain effect on sales. The hyperscaler spending outlook, especially the updated calendar 2027 assessment, supports the ramp, but gives no Vera allocation.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json\n112:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Microsoft states no calendar-2026 total in the evidence. Its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% year over year, and its $678bn Azure backlog supports a continuing ramp: carrying the growth pace through the year at roughly 15% sequential increases a quarter gives about $155bn on the reported quarter's basis (not the flat annualised $124bn). The outside tallies of the four companies' guided 2026 spending ($720-760bn) less the other three companies' guidance ($540-570bn) imply roughly $175-190bn at their centres, and those tallies include spending (such as leases) the single reported quarter may not. I take $175bn, between the two.\",\n245:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\n276:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json\n207:      \"basis_text\": \"I expect combined spending to land near the high end of the Zacks range. The Alphabet, Amazon and Meta revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support a strong pace. Amazon's higher memory costs also lift dollar spending. Project delays and grid connection constraints lead me to assume that some planned spending slips, keeping the forecast below the higher published plan.\",\n226:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to its total capex guidance. I use that comparison as evidence of a high share and allow for other capital spending across all four companies. The packet gives no group-wide data-center split.\",\n257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total capex. Project delays and grid constraints temper the pace, and the additional AWS GPU deployment is scheduled after CY2026. Applying my 90% data-center share to the total puts the forecast below $700 billion. The spending mix is the biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n93:      \"basis_text\": \"My forecast of FY2028 CPU revenue growth over FY2027 is 1.8x. Management's outlook of more than 2x (F2) is a full-year target for the next fiscal year: all four selling quarters are still ahead, and it rests on a Vera ramp that no passage yet shows in volume. So it is discounted for time and execution. Anthropic is only evaluating Vera (e5044), deployments are described in the future tense (e4689), and AMD claims its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404, a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal). The lower anchor is the outside market pace of about 50% a year (F4; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 in e4857 is a similar pace), which is what a vendor that only holds its share would reach (1.5x). NVIDIA's claim of deployment at every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above that pace. 1.8x sits between the market pace (1.5x) and the guidance floor (2.0x), weighted toward the guidance because the CFO reaffirmed it.\",\n131:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate Vera at 90% of NVIDIA's FY2028 CPU revenue. Management attributes the FY2028 doubling to Vera itself (e4691: 'Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year'; e4856), and Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689), so Vera is most of the year's CPU revenue. The remainder is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. No passage sizes that remainder. The rung holds for any share from about 84% up to 100% at the forecast pace.\",\n162:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 3 (Neutral, $30-40 billion). I forecast about $32 billion of Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 (F8). Reference: NVIDIA's own outlook. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and said FY2028 should more than double it (e3660). Huang and other outlets carry the same claim (e4764, e4691, e4856), counted once. That puts the reference above $40 billion (F3), at rung 2. No consensus figure is supplied. How I got the outcome: the FY2027 base is current-year guidance, so I take it in full (F1). The FY2028 doubling is a full-year target for the next fiscal year. All four selling quarters are still ahead, and it depends on a Vera ramp that the evidence does not yet show in volume: Anthropic is still evaluating Vera (e5044), and deployments are described in the future tense (e4689). So the target is discounted for time and execution rather than taken as the answer. Bounds on the growth pace: a vendor that only held share in a server-CPU market growing about 50% a year (AMD, e4687; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 is a similar pace, e4857) would grow about 1.5x, giving $30 billion. The guidance floor is 2.0x, giving $40 billion. Broad deployment claims (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above the market pace. AMD's benchmark claims that its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404; a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal) argue against taking the guidance in full. I forecast 1.8x (F5), which gives $36 billion of total CPU revenue (F6). The question asks only about Vera. Management attributes the doubling to Vera (e4691, e4856), so I put Vera at 90% of FY2028 CPU revenue (F7). The rest is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. That gives $32.4 billion. Sensitivity: rung 3 holds for any Vera share from about 84% to 100% at this pace. Reaching rung 2 needs the full guided 2x pace with nearly all CPU revenue being Vera. Falling to rung 4 needs a pace below about 1.67x at a 90% share. Scope note: two outlets describe the guidance as total CPU revenue (e3660, e4764) and two as Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856); the share figure covers that gap. Themes: our capex judgements put hyperscaler data-center spending at about $750 billion in 2026 (v6) and above $1.1 trillion in 2027 (v7, updated upward). That supports demand and keeps the pace well above the market's own growth, but on its own it does not supply the share gain that a pace above 2x needs.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n94:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that NVIDIA realizes 90% of the reference. The announced breadth of deployments supports most of the ramp. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, and AMD's agentic-workload tests show a competitive alternative. I allow for some planned sales to slip or go to a rival, equivalent to less than half an average quarter of revenue at the target pace. The passages give no order sizes or shipment pace to narrow that estimate.\",\n130:      \"reasons\": \"Management's doubling outlook gives a $40 billion reference at the lower edge of rung 2. The Vera-specific reporting ties that outlook to the $20 billion FY2027 CPU baseline. Separate reports of potential to more than double describe the same outlook and leave the upside unsized. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and rising hyperscaler spending supports demand. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, while AMD's own benchmarks show a credible alternative for agentic workloads. Those benchmarks indicate competitive risk without quantifying lost sales. I expect most of the planned ramp to land, with a modest shortfall from deployment timing and customer choice. Realizing 90% of the reference puts my FY2028 forecast at $36 billion, within rung 3.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\n188:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of Microsoft's calendar-2026 capital expenditure; no calendar-year guidance is shown. The January-March 2026 quarter (fiscal Q3) was $30.88bn, up 84% from a year earlier (F5, F6). A continued sequential rise of about 16% a quarter, the pace that compounds to roughly 84% over a year and is supported by the $678bn Azure backlog (F7), gives about 30.9 + 35.8 + 41.6 + 48.2 = $156bn; the four-company tally less the three guided companies implies $150-205bn (F9). $165bn follows the run-rate path and moves partway into the tally-implied range, staying in its lower part because the run-rate is the only Microsoft-specific number.\",\n264:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending. The companies' indicated AI-infrastructure total, more than $730bn (F12), is at or above the total-capex tally of $720-745bn (F8), which puts the share near 100%; Meta's planned AI capex of $130bn (F13) sits inside its $125-145bn total guidance (F2), about 96% of the midpoint; Amazon's raise to $220bn was mostly memory-chip costs, i.e. server hardware (e1649); outside estimates put spending on data centers at about $750bn (e1416, e4082). No passage sizes non-data-center spending such as offices or logistics, so I keep a 2% residual; the passages bound the share between about 96% and 100%.\",\n390:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: outside tallies put the four companies' combined 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; about $745bn tracking, e1856), inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Build: the three companies with full-year guidance add to $540-570bn (F4): Alphabet $195-205bn after raising from $180-190bn (e1254), Meta $125-145bn after a raise (e1549), Amazon $220bn after raising from $200bn (e1649). Microsoft gives no calendar-year guidance in the evidence; its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and a continued ramp of about 16% a quarter gives about $156bn, while the tally less the other three implies $150-205bn; I use $165bn (F10, my estimate). Total capital expenditure is therefore about $720bn (F11; $705-735bn across the guidance ranges). The question asks for data-center spending, which is narrower than total capex, so I apply a 98% data-center share (F14, my estimate): the companies indicated more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending (e1947), at or above the total tally; Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) sits inside its $125-145bn guidance; Amazon's raise was mostly memory chips; outside estimates put spending on data centers near $750bn (e1416, e4082). The share is bounded at about 96-100% by these passages, giving data-center capex of about $706bn (F15), rung 4; the supported floor from e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure) also lies in rung 4. Rung 5 is not supported: the $795-800bn figures (NVIDIA, e2759; BofA, e5055) cover five companies, Oracle among them in BofA's list, so the four sit below $800bn, and the highest four-company tally is $760bn (e2458). Pushing up: three mid-year guidance raises, rising memory costs that lift the dollar cost of the same build (e1649), and strong cloud demand (Azure backlog $678bn, e4405; Google Cloud +82%, e4406; AWS's best quarter in four years, e4404). Pushing down: about $130bn of US data-center projects blocked or delayed this year (e122, not specific to these four), and grid-connection and electrician constraints (e5292, e1545); the memory shortage (e4566) limits volume but raises price, so it cuts both ways. The forecast sits close to the $700bn edge, so the main risk is rung 3 if Microsoft's ramp or the data-center share comes in lower; the repeated raises make an undershoot of guidance less likely. The many reprints of NVIDIA's top-five figure count as one claim (e4564's '$1.3 billion' for 2027 is a misprint of $1.3 trillion).\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\n207:      \"basis_text\": \"I forecast spending at the upper end of the Zacks range. Alphabet, Amazon and Meta lifted their full-year plans, and Microsoft's reported quarter and strong cloud demand support a rising spending pace. Amazon's higher memory costs lift dollar spending even if equipment volumes rise more slowly. Project delays and grid constraints make the higher plan tally less likely to be fully spent this year. The annual estimates already reflect the company plans, so I use those plans to choose where spending lands within the estimates.\",\n226:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate a $25 billion deduction for spending outside data centers. The four-company AI infrastructure tally is close to my total-capex forecast, and Meta's AI capex estimate is close to the midpoint of its companywide guidance. Those comparisons support a small deduction. I allow room for different spending mixes across the companies and for some AI infrastructure spending to fall outside data centers. The passages do not give a company-by-company split.\",\n257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $720 billion of calendar 2026 data-center capex, in rung 4. The closest market reference, more than $730 billion of AI infrastructure spending for the same four companies, points to rung 4 and uses a broader spending label. Raised company outlooks, Microsoft's reported spending and strong cloud demand support $745 billion of total capex. After my $25 billion deduction for spending outside data centers, the forecast stays above $700 billion. Grid constraints and delayed projects put the lower boundary at risk, but the delay tally covers U.S. projects across developers and gives no amount attributable to these four companies' 2026 capex. NVIDIA's nearly $800 billion estimate covers five companies and serves as a cross-check. The AWS GPU expansion is scheduled for 2027–2028 and gives no amount to add to 2026.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/S/schema.json; do echo \"$f\"; rg -in -C 5 '\"'estimated|qualifier' \\\"\"'$f\"; done\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '\"'16,24p'\npython3 -Bc 'from pathlib import Path; import json; d=Path(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23\\\"); k=json.loads((d/\\\"reader-key.json\\\").read_text()); [print(p,json.loads((d/v[\\\"answer\\\"]).read_text())[\\\"answers\\\"]) for p,v in k.items() if p in (\\\"U01\\\",\\\"U03\\\",\\\"U04\\\")]; print((d.parent/\\\"judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22\\\"/\\\"reread2-key.json\\\").read_text())'\nrg -n 'fiscal|guidance of|midpoint|\"'^Question'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/user.txt | head -16\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
