{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -Bc 'from pathlib import Path;import json; b=Path(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23\\\"); k=json.loads((b/\\\"reader-key.json\\\").read_text()); print(\\\"U04 and U01 retained figures\\\"); \nfor n in [\\\"U04\\\",\\\"U01\\\",\\\"U03\\\"]:\n print(n)\n for f in json.loads((b/k[n][\\\"answer\\\"]).read_text())[\\\"figures\\\"]:\n  print(f[\\\"id\\\"],f.get(\\\"label\\\"),f.get(\\\"value\\\"),f.get(\\\"low\\\"),f.get(\\\"high\\\"),f.get(\\\"basis_text\\\") or \\\"\\\")'\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json | sed -n '192,305p'\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.claude.json | cut -c1-2800\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json | sed -n '196,216p'\nrg -n 'D747' docs/{CURRENT_STATE,DECISIONS}.md | tail -8\ntail -3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md | cut -c1-320\ncmp -s poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/E/user.txt && echo same-E-user\ncmp -s poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/S/user.txt && echo same-S-user\ndiff -u poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3-2026-11-22,4-2026-11-23}/E/system.txt | cut -c1-400\"","aggregated_output":"U04 and U01 retained figures\nU04\nF1 None None 720 745 \nF2 None 760 None None \nF3 None None 195 205 \nF4 None 220 None None \nF5 None None 125 145 \nF6 None 130 None None \nF7 None 30.88 None None \nF8 None 84 None None \nF9 None 730 None None \nF10 None 800 None None \nF11 None 745 None None I expect combined spending to land near the high end of the Zacks range. The Alphabet, Amazon and Meta revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support a strong pace. Amazon's higher memory costs also lift dollar spending. Project delays and grid connection constraints lead me to assume that some planned spending slips, keeping the forecast below the higher published plan.\nF12 None 90 None None I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to its total capex guidance. I use that comparison as evidence of a high share and allow for other capital spending across all four companies. The packet gives no group-wide data-center split.\nF13 None 670.5 None None \nU01\nF1 None 75.25 None None \nF2 None 89.0 None None \nF3 None 164.25 None None \nF4 None 96.2 None None \nF5 None 91.0 None None \nF6 None 5.71 None None \nF7 None 65 None None \nF8 None 68 None None \nF9 None 4.62 None None \nF10 None 108.0 None None \nF11 None 5.0 None None My estimate. NVIDIA beat its revenue guidance midpoint by 5.7% in FY2027 Q2 (F6) and by 4.6% in FY2026 Q4 (F9), and has beaten the high end of guidance in each of the last 12 quarters, by 3.1% on average over the last four (about 5% above the midpoint; e5054, e5055). Capacity is sold out, which limits further upside (e4911), so I assume a 5% beat on the $108.0 billion Q3 midpoint, not more than the recent record.\nF12 None 113.4 None None \nF13 None 12.0 None None My estimate of the Q4 sequential pace, benchmarked on the 12% sequential growth the Q3 guidance implies at its midpoint (e5005). Upward forces: NVIDIA says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e5061, e4283), supply and capacity commitments jumped to $279 billion (e2759), and Vera Rubin carries more revenue per gigawatt (e4202). Limits: supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays constrained through FY2028 (e3733, e5067), and my Q3 base already includes a beat. So I keep the guided 12% pace rather than the 17-18% delivered in recent quarters. The pace leaves room for limited H200 sales to China late in the year if purchases are authorized; no passage sizes such sales, so no separate figure is made.\nF14 None 127.01 None None \nF15 None 240.41 None None \nF16 None 92.8 None None My estimate. Data Center was 92.5% of revenue in FY2027 Q2 ($89.0 billion of $96.2 billion; F2, F4) and its share has risen every quarter, from 87.9% to 92.5%, with the latest step only 0.3 points (e3399). Growth in Q3 is driven primarily by the AI data center business (e5223), so I assume the share keeps edging up, to an average of 92.8% for H2.\nF17 None 223.1 None None \nF18 None 387.35 None None \nF19 None 361 None None \nF20 None 81.62 None None \nF21 None 177.82 None None \nF22 None 418.23 None None \nF23 None 411 None None \nU03\nF1 None 20 None None \nF2 None 2 None None \nF3 None 40 None None \nF4 None 85 None None I estimate that Vera realizes most of the stated CPU opportunity in its initial ramp. Expected deployment across major customers supports a high share, while the evidence gives no sales volumes to justify assigning the entire CPU opportunity to realized Vera revenue.\nF5 None 17 None None \nF6 None 2 None None I estimate that Vera revenue doubles from the discounted launch-year base as deployments broaden. Management's expansion outlook sets the benchmark for the pace. Anthropic is evaluating Vera, and AMD's platform and agentic-workload benchmarks suggest competition for additional deployments.\nF7 None 34 None None \n   192\t    },\n   193\t    {\n   194\t      \"id\": \"F11\",\n   195\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   196\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   197\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   198\t      \"value\": 720,\n   199\t      \"low\": null,\n   200\t      \"high\": null,\n   201\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   202\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   203\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   204\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   205\t      \"inputs\": [\"F4\", \"F10\"],\n   206\t      \"operation\": \"sum\",\n   207\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   208\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   209\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   210\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   211\t    },\n   212\t    {\n   213\t      \"id\": \"F12\",\n   214\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft and Alphabet\", \"measure\": \"AI infrastructure spending\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   215\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   216\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n   217\t      \"value\": 730,\n   218\t      \"low\": null,\n   219\t      \"high\": null,\n   220\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   221\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1947\",\n   222\t      \"quote_number\": \"$730 billion\",\n   223\t      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n   224\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   225\t      \"operation\": null,\n   226\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   227\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   228\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   229\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   230\t    },\n   231\t    {\n   232\t      \"id\": \"F13\",\n   233\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Meta Platforms\", \"measure\": \"AI capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"total\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   234\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   235\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   236\t      \"value\": 130,\n   237\t      \"low\": null,\n   238\t      \"high\": null,\n   239\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   240\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e3686\",\n   241\t      \"quote_number\": \"$130 billion\",\n   242\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   243\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   244\t      \"operation\": null,\n   245\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   246\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   247\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   248\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   249\t    },\n   250\t    {\n   251\t      \"id\": \"F14\",\n   252\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   253\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n   254\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   255\t      \"value\": 98,\n   256\t      \"low\": null,\n   257\t      \"high\": null,\n   258\t      \"unit\": \"%\",\n   259\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   260\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   261\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   262\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   263\t      \"operation\": null,\n   264\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending. The companies' indicated AI-infrastructure total, more than $730bn (F12), is at or above the total-capex tally of $720-745bn (F8), which puts the share near 100%; Meta's planned AI capex of $130bn (F13) sits inside its $125-145bn total guidance (F2), about 96% of the midpoint; Amazon's raise to $220bn was mostly memory-chip costs, i.e. server hardware (e1649); outside estimates put spending on data centers at about $750bn (e1416, e4082). No passage sizes non-data-center spending such as offices or logistics, so I keep a 2% residual; the passages bound the share between about 96% and 100%.\",\n   265\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F12\", \"F8\", \"F13\", \"F2\", \"e1649\", \"e1416\", \"e4082\"],\n   266\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   267\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   268\t    },\n   269\t    {\n   270\t      \"id\": \"F15\",\n   271\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   272\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   273\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   274\t      \"value\": 705.6,\n   275\t      \"low\": null,\n   276\t      \"high\": null,\n   277\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   278\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   279\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   280\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   281\t      \"inputs\": [\"F11\", \"F14\"],\n   282\t      \"operation\": \"share\",\n   283\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   284\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   285\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v6\"],\n   286\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   287\t    },\n   288\t    {\n   289\t      \"id\": \"F16\",\n   290\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   291\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   292\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   293\t      \"value\": 745,\n   294\t      \"low\": null,\n   295\t      \"high\": null,\n   296\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   297\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1856\",\n   298\t      \"quote_number\": \"$745 billion\",\n   299\t      \"qualifier\": \"near\",\n   300\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   301\t      \"operation\": null,\n   302\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   303\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   304\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   305\t      \"reference_for\": []\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The answer says no passage gives the four companies' data-center share, so it cannot tell whether spending is above or below $700bn (about 94% of total capex). That gap does not survive the strongest passages. The ceiling is firm: data-center capex cannot exceed the four companies' total capex, which is 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' (e5014), 'near $745 billion' (e1856) and '$760 billion' (e2458). All of these are below $800bn. For the floor, e1947 names the same four firms: 'Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year.' The answer calls this 'a broader AI category' but cites no passage for that, and no passage describes AI infrastructure as reaching beyond data-center spending. Every other passage points the same way. e4082 puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year', matching the four-firm totals. e5376 puts five-firm 'data center capital expenditures' at 'nearly $800 billion', against five-firm total capex of 'roughly $795 billion' (e5055), which is a share of about 100%. Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against its '$125 billion to $145 billion' guidance (e1549) is about 96% at the midpoint, above the 94% line the answer names. No passage sizes non-data-center capex large enough to pull the share below about 94%. The system text says a rung is 'the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely ... not the one it has proved' and that 'not established' is 'never a reason to stay'. The answer's reason is a lack of confirmation, not a real gap: the passages make rung 4 the most likely outcome, though not a proven one. The answer's own 90% share (F12, giving F13 = 670.5) has no passage behind it and is left unused, so it does not keep the rung open.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n   196\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   197\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   198\t      \"value\": 750,\n   199\t      \"low\": null,\n   200\t      \"high\": null,\n   201\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   202\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1416\",\n   203\t      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\n   204\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   205\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   206\t      \"operation\": null,\n   207\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   208\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   209\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   210\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   211\t    },\n   212\t    {\n   213\t      \"id\": \"F12\",\n   214\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"five largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   215\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   216\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1090:| D746 | 2026-11-23 | D745 targeted test FAILS: E's Codex reply settled not supported (an unsized 10% cut decides the band); S's two replies, E's Claude reply and the justified-unresolved control split along model family (8 of 8 adjudicator votes); P-v8 2 of 2; 0 of 6 withheld; council 61–61b: ask Alex one general question (may a forecast rest on an explained indirect comparison, or must it wait until the passages settle the deciding side; lean: allow it, explained), then one $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words; the paid re-send stays on hold until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test; question answered in D747 | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1091:| D747 | 2026-11-23 | Alex: \"可以发布，要讲清楚\" — a forecast may rest on a related figure when the passages do not give the asked quantity, supported only when the answer explains why the comparison applies to the companies, period and quantity asked and why, under its own stated assumptions, that side of the line is more likely; D741 ruling 3 and D745's unresolved ruling still hold; next: the words into the shared reader definitions, then one $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers after council review | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:448:**The one next task: the $0 blinded re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (D747), after council round 62 reviews it.** Alex ruled\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:449:(D747, \"可以发布，要讲清楚\") that a forecast may rest on a related figure when the passages do not give the asked quantity. It is\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:454:- The re-read is registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D747]: nine stored answers, four fixed readings and five that must settle.\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:457:The record of the D746 step (Alex answered its question in D747):\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:459:**Earlier next task (done, D747): Alex answers one general question (D746).** \"When the passages do not give the asked number, and\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\nsame-E-user\nsame-S-user\n--- poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/system.txt\t2026-11-22 20:23:53\n+++ poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt\t2026-11-22 23:34:07\n@@ -1,9 +1,9 @@\n You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, \n \n - Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n-- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported f\n+- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported f\n - Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarter\n - An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n - \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it\n - Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n-- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n+- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a for\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"agent_message","text":"I found two ways the re-read could give a misleading result. The tally accepts matching votes without checking their reasons, and the format instructions for the two older answers include a word their writers couldn’t use. I’ll check both against the saved material."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B - <<'PY'\nimport ast, io, json, contextlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\nbase=Path(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\\\")\nb4=Path(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23\\\")\nb3=b4.parent/\\\"judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22\\\"\nprint(\\\"Stored U03 equals third-run E:\\\",json.loads((b4/\\\"controls/K-unsupported.json\\\").read_text())==json.loads((b3/\\\"E/answers/codex.json\\\").read_text()))\nfor b in (b3,b4):\n for c in (\\\"S\\\",\\\"E\\\"):\n  schema=json.loads((b/c/\\\"schema.json\\\").read_text())\n  print(b.name,c,\\\"estimated\\\" in schema[\\\"properties\\\"][\\\"figures\\\"][\\\"items\\\"][\\\"properties\\\"][\\\"qualifier\\\"][\\\"anyOf\\\"][0][\\\"enum\\\"])\ntree=ast.parse((base/\\\"blind4.py\\\").read_text())\ntree.body=[n for n in tree.body if isinstance(n,ast.FunctionDef) and n.name in (\\\"reread_tally\\\",\\\"_defect_key\\\") or isinstance(n,ast.Assign) and isinstance(n.targets[0],ast.Name) and n.targets[0].id==\\\"REREAD_EXPECTED\\\"]\nenv=dict(Path=Path,json=json)\nexec(compile(tree,\\\"blind4.py\\\",\\\"exec\\\"),env)\nlabels=[\\\"blind4 U01\\\",\\\"blind4 U02\\\",\\\"blind4 U03\\\",\\\"blind4 U04\\\",\\\"blind4 U05\\\",\\\"blind4 U06\\\",\\\"blind4 U08\\\",\\\"blind3 T10\\\",\\\"blind3 T15\\\"]\nkey={f\\\"fake-{i}\\\":dict(label=label,answer=\\\"fake-answer\\\",case=\\\"test\\\",writer=\\\"test\\\") for i,label in enumerate(labels)}\ndef reading(p):\n value=env[\\\"REREAD_EXPECTED\\\"].get(key[p.name.split(\\\".\\\")[0]][\\\"label\\\"],\\\"yes\\\")\n return {\\\"members\\\":{\\\"v\\\":{\\\"supported\\\":value,\\\"ground\\\":\\\"AI spending is a guaranteed floor\\\" if \\\"codex\\\" in p.name else \\\"AI spending supplies no floor\\\"}},\\\"defects\\\":[]}\nenv[\\\"_reading\\\"]=reading\ndef read(p):\n return json.dumps(key if p.name.endswith(\\\"key.json\\\") else {\\\"answers\\\":[{\\\"view_id\\\":\\\"v\\\",\\\"shape\\\":\\\"forecast\\\"}]})\nfor mode in (\\\"opposed reasons\\\",\\\"empty population\\\"):\n if mode==\\\"empty population\\\":key={}\n with patch.object(Path,\\\"exists\\\",return_value=False),patch.object(Path,\\\"read_text\\\",read),patch.object(Path,\\\"write_text\\\",lambda p,s:None),contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()) as output:\n  env[\\\"reread_tally\\\"](Path(\\\"simulation\\\"))\n print(mode,output.getvalue().splitlines()[-1])\nPY\nrg -n 'T10|F11|estimated' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | tail -10\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt | sed -n '17,18p'\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt | sed -n '26,30p;43,48p;127,129p'\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11559,11569p'\nsed -n '93,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
